00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:34.095
the best thing to do is to actually dream
up a course and offer it.

00:06:34.095 --> 00:06:37.431
[LAUGH]

00:06:37.431 --> 00:06:41.830
So, when you dream up a course and offer
it, a couple of things happen.

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:45.960
The first thing is, you are super
motivated, cuz you're clueless.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:49.122
You have to read a lot, you've gotta put
together something.

00:06:49.122 --> 00:06:52.843
And the students they, you know, they
sense your excitement and your

00:06:52.843 --> 00:06:56.990
curiosity, they kind of engage with you
and they teach you a lot.

00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:58.995
And, so we were kind of thinking, how

00:06:58.995 --> 00:07:01.577
do we teach people about how to scale
something?

00:07:01.577 --> 00:07:05.830
And Bob and I thought, let's give them a
challenging, hard problem.

00:07:05.830 --> 00:07:09.900
So, we had 60 students, we could have
gotten more but we capped it at 60.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:13.010
30 from engineering and 30 from GSB.

00:07:13.010 --> 00:07:17.230
And then we put them into teams and
randomly assigned dorms and

00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:21.280
spokes team on the Stanford campus to each
of these student teams.

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:24.890
And we give them one scaling challenge.

00:07:24.890 --> 00:07:29.870
Scale to use of bicycle helmets by
bicyclists on Stanford campus.

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:33.860
It's a very hard thing to do by the way.

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:39.450
because, you know, there's memory loss and
particularly the undergrads are fully

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:42.712
pursuant of their immortality and
invincibility

00:07:42.712 --> 00:07:45.710
[LAUGH] and it was very, very hard.

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:48.480
But the students came up with like,
amazing ideas.

00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:52.037
You know, so one team I remember they were
given

00:07:52.037 --> 00:07:56.240
EV, they scandled a village with these you
know, grad students.

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:59.720
And so they kinda went to the grad
students and try to persuade them

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:03.490
and facts and, you know, here's what
happens when you fall of a bike.

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:05.200
None of these things worked.

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:07.400
And finally these people said wait a
minute.

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:11.650
We really ought a go to the elementary
school on the Stanford campus.

00:08:11.650 --> 00:08:14.460
Because a lot of the kids of grad students
actually go there.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:17.650
Let's actually organize a campaign amongst
these kids.

00:08:17.650 --> 00:08:20.480
And what they did was, they actually got
the kids to

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:23.970
write little notes for their parents,
saying hey, wear a helmet.

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:25.650
It was amazing.

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:29.950
And by the way, the way we graded the
course was, we knew what the base rate

00:08:29.950 --> 00:08:32.230
of helmet usage was, so the more you

00:08:32.230 --> 00:08:34.630
moved the needle, the better the grade,
you know?

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:36.830
Which caused some interesting challenges,
I might add.

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:42.390
[LAUGH] And so there was another team that
went to the men's soccer, team.

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:46.660
So, they told him look, you know, if you
fall off a bike, bad things can happen,

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:48.300
you know, you're telling the men's soccer
team,

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:50.670
they just said give me a break, you know.

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:52.420
But, then, they quickly realized, wait a

00:08:52.420 --> 00:08:54.800
minute, we need to communicate this to
them

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:58.580
in an interesting way, and they came

00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:01.050
with the idea of a watermelon smashing
competition.

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:04.380
So they would go there, and tell these
people after they dropped

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:08.550
a water melon, that's your brain when you
fall off a bike.

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:09.750
And people said, really.

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:12.140
And they said, you know, you may think
you're immortal but if

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:14.270
that happens to you, think of what's gonna
happen to your team.

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:17.810
And that got the attention of the men's
soccer team.

00:09:19.550 --> 00:09:23.600
And very quickly you know, their penalty
if you will, if.

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:25.787
Any of the men's soccer team didn't wear a

00:09:25.787 --> 00:09:29.940
bike helmet, they'd actually fling the
watermelon at the kid.

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:32.430
So, you know, that kind of deviant
behavior got punished.

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:34.160
It sort of spread for awhile.

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:35.730
And the second year we actually did a

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:38.350
project with the MBAs and the engineers
again, and

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:40.250
this time they were actually working with
front

00:09:40.250 --> 00:09:42.439
line engineers and soldiers and that kind
of stuff.

00:09:44.990 --> 00:09:45.780
It's unbelievable.

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:49.390
I mean when you sort of see we actually
had a general in

00:09:49.390 --> 00:09:53.830
the rapid deployment force who came to us
and said hey can they help.

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:55.700
And we said oh, what a great problem.

00:09:55.700 --> 00:10:00.230
And so we once again told our students to
go figure these things out.

00:10:00.230 --> 00:10:04.790
And the sorts of things they came up with
were, like, simple but profound too.

00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:06.125
One, just one example.

00:10:06.125 --> 00:10:09.285
[COUGH]

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:12.450
So soldiers, of course, get redeployed,
and

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:14.880
you get rotated and you have replacements.

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:17.490
And you have interesting transfer of
knowledge problem.

00:10:17.490 --> 00:10:21.180
How do you transfer knowledge from
outgoing cohort of soldiers.

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:22.800
To an incoming cohort.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:26.145
The U.S Army's modus operandi is, you
actually have a

00:10:26.145 --> 00:10:29.640
two hundred item questionnaire that you
have to fill out.

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:31.030
And, you can imagine what people are going
to do

00:10:31.030 --> 00:10:33.210
when you give a two hundred item
questionnaire and that's

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:34.860
given to you right after you land on the
air

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:37.170
base in the U.S. when you've come back
from Kandahar.

00:10:38.570 --> 00:10:41.630
You know, the response rate is abysmally
low.

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:44.070
So, there's just no way you're going to
get anything.

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:46.280
Plus it goes to some place in the
Pentagon.

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:50.400
They do some statistical analyses and they
don't turn out to be much either.

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:53.670
So our kids actually thought about for
awhile and they came up with like a,

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:57.960
one interesting idea that they came up
with for example was a look, why don't we.

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:02.290
Ask each soldier.

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:05.320
Look, can you take ten minutes to talk to
someone who is going to replace you.

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:11.960
Tell them three stories that actually are
interesting examples of your experience.

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:14.100
And if you spend ten minutes, we'll make
sure you get

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:17.110
half an hour more web access when you talk
to your family.

00:11:18.650 --> 00:11:21.910
But, by the way, it's a very high powered
incentive, as you can image.

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:25.266
Web access is a very scarce resource, you
give them half an hour, and you

00:11:25.266 --> 00:11:27.576
give these stories, and it's amazing when
these

00:11:27.576 --> 00:11:30.069
people would tell stories to the incoming
soldiers.

00:11:30.069 --> 00:11:32.521
What they were doing is they were actually
giving way

00:11:32.521 --> 00:11:35.630
more data can, than can be accommodated by
a regression model.

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:39.310
You know you sort of say you know, sunny
days, sun

00:11:39.310 --> 00:11:43.210
on the arise, the enemies in the hills or
what ever.

00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:46.620
Just look at the number of variables that
are being incorporated in that story.

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:49.592
And it is very officially packaged.

00:11:49.592 --> 00:11:51.650
So the army is actually trying it out.

00:11:51.650 --> 00:11:55.190
So any way after many years of trying it
out we sort of thought you know.

00:11:55.190 --> 00:11:58.980
We've actually learned, and we interviewed
a number of people

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:01.720
we wrote case studies, we did a bunch of
things.

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:05.052
So six years after the original question,

00:12:05.052 --> 00:12:08.670
we actually thought okay let's write this
book.

00:12:08.670 --> 00:12:11.260
And first a word about excellence.

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:16.180
You know, we could debate this to what
excellence is you know.

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:18.690
Plato says this or Socrates says this.

00:12:18.690 --> 00:12:21.980
If your interested in eastern philosophy
the Gita says this.

00:12:21.980 --> 00:12:26.560
But Bob and i are Social scientist and for
us excellence simply mean

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:30.040
people do the right job even when nobody
is looking over your shoulder.

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:31.810
That's what excellence is.

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:35.250
Because when you need people to monitor
others executing a task.

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:35.930
That's not excellence.

00:12:37.056 --> 00:12:39.062
You know, for every ten people whom

00:12:39.062 --> 00:12:42.300
you hire, you can't have three cops
monitoring.

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:43.860
There is no way you can scale if you do
that.

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:49.590
Cuz it's just you know, going to be huge
in bureaucratic and expenses.

00:12:49.590 --> 00:12:51.900
So what's the problem of excellence?

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:54.080
There are two dimensions to the problem.

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:54.970
On the one hand.

00:12:55.990 --> 00:12:58.010
You, if you're in a large corporation, or
even a

00:12:58.010 --> 00:13:01.760
small one, you might have a pocket of
goodness, somewhere.

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:05.190
And what your challenge as a manager is,
or as an executive or

00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:09.900
leader is, is how do I actually construct
a dominant chain of goodness?

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:12.840
How do I spread goodness from one place to
the others?

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:18.060
The challenge is when you do that you have
voltage loss.

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:18.770
This dilution.

00:13:19.850 --> 00:13:23.440
There's kind of another dimension of
scaling up accidents and that is if you're

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:26.250
a small organization and as you're adding

00:13:26.250 --> 00:13:28.660
people how do you actually scale up
accidents.

00:13:29.900 --> 00:13:32.900
So, you know, wonders if you will
horizontal or cross

00:13:32.900 --> 00:13:37.920
divisional or cross business unit, the
others, kinda, much more temporal.

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:41.550
There's also a little problem and the
little problem is, the larger an

00:13:41.550 --> 00:13:45.220
organization gets, the more likely are
smart

00:13:45.220 --> 00:13:47.770
people, inside the organization, to become
dumber.

00:13:49.670 --> 00:13:50.950
And I don't say this to be coy.

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:55.820
And what I mean by beep smart people
becoming dumber is, I don't mean to

00:13:55.820 --> 00:13:58.180
imply, obviously, that they become stupid,
but I

00:13:58.180 --> 00:14:01.430
certainly mean to imply that they become
silent.

00:14:01.430 --> 00:14:04.120
And that's the problem in a large
organization.

00:14:04.120 --> 00:14:05.740
If they become silent.

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:08.310
You're basically in trouble.

00:14:08.310 --> 00:14:11.071
So lots of examples of scaling you know,

00:14:11.071 --> 00:14:14.430
a lovely little start up called Pulse, you
know.

00:14:14.430 --> 00:14:17.724
They would always, they were two Stanford
grads, and they would call us

00:14:17.724 --> 00:14:21.036
and say, we have a scaling problem, I'd
say like, what kind of problem?

00:14:21.036 --> 00:14:24.740
There's just the two of us, we've hired
four more people, we have scaling problem.

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:29.013
I'm like, you guys are all working in one
room, how could that be a scaling problem?

00:14:29.013 --> 00:14:30.953
But sure enough you realize they needed

00:14:30.953 --> 00:14:33.870
to coordinate, they needed to set a
communicate.

00:14:33.870 --> 00:14:35.855
And on the other extreme, you have

00:14:35.855 --> 00:14:39.610
the institute for health improvement in
Cambridge.

00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:42.611
They want to reduce debts caused by
preventable medical errors.

00:14:42.611 --> 00:14:44.413
100,000 people a year die.

00:14:44.413 --> 00:14:48.001
How do you do that when you don't have
cloud, when you don't have the quality?

00:14:48.001 --> 00:14:51.930
When you don't have resources, and you
have 3,500 hospitals?

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:54.436
So that gives you if you will, the breadth
of the.

00:14:54.436 --> 00:14:56.986
Scaling challenge.

00:14:56.986 --> 00:15:02.884
So, the, the main sort of point I wanna I
'd suggest is.

00:15:02.884 --> 00:15:07.210
Scaling up is deceptively easy but
screwing up is easier.

00:15:07.210 --> 00:15:13.340
And the term, the scientific term Bob and
I use is, we call it a clusterfug.

00:15:13.340 --> 00:15:16.468
And we can't of course use the actual
word.

00:15:16.468 --> 00:15:18.128
And so what we did was we,

00:15:18.128 --> 00:15:21.741
suddenly remembered, wait a minute, Norman
Mailer wrote

00:15:21.741 --> 00:15:25.064
about fug successfully, and so we thought
let's

00:15:25.064 --> 00:15:28.388
actually exploit this literally lineage of
the term,

00:15:28.388 --> 00:15:34.200
so we called it clusterfug, and writing
the case as we speak, On the Navy Seals,

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:36.790
yeah I know, can you imagine a guy like me
riding a case on Navy Seals.

00:15:38.070 --> 00:15:41.850
I know, even I've chuckled about the
absurdity of this.

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:44.048
So, I spend [INAUDIBLE] we actually have
two

00:15:44.048 --> 00:15:47.140
amazing students who are helping me write
this case.

00:15:47.140 --> 00:15:49.939
Carter [UNKNOWN], who's a Navy Seal
himself,

00:15:49.939 --> 00:15:53.350
and Gib Lopez, who's actually an MBA also.

00:15:53.350 --> 00:15:56.620
Both of them are helping me write this
case, and Carter and I went and.

00:15:56.620 --> 00:15:59.380
Spent a chunk of time at Coronado.

00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:01.050
In, but I was with the Navy Seal
commander.

00:16:01.050 --> 00:16:02.900
You know they use a lot of abbreviations.

00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:05.490
And there was one abbreviation that kind
of baffled me.

00:16:05.490 --> 00:16:06.600
They called it PFM.

00:16:07.610 --> 00:16:11.940
And after awhile I sort of had pretty good
idea of what the F stood for.

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:13.640
I'm sure all of you know that.

00:16:13.640 --> 00:16:17.850
It was the P and the M that I was actually
utterly befuddled by.

00:16:17.850 --> 00:16:21.810
And later when I asked the commanding
officer, I said what is PFM?

00:16:21.810 --> 00:16:28.070
He said, pure, you of course know what the
f is, and the m, of course, means madness.

00:16:28.070 --> 00:16:34.530
He said, that's what happens to us when we
try and scale sometimes, PFM.

00:16:34.530 --> 00:16:37.362
Now, if you really look at, 30 years of
social

00:16:37.362 --> 00:16:41.240
psychology, why is it that scaling ends up
in a clusterfug?

00:16:41.240 --> 00:16:42.870
And this could be any decision.

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:47.470
New product, new technology
implementation, an acquisition, expansion

00:16:47.470 --> 00:16:52.830
overseas, whatever the articulation or
realization of scaling.

00:16:52.830 --> 00:16:55.740
It turns into a clusterfug because of
three things.

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:57.120
The first is.

00:16:57.120 --> 00:16:58.800
Sense of illusion.

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:02.260
You actually have illusion because the
smart people have become silent.

00:17:02.260 --> 00:17:04.880
You're not getting any feedback and it's
kind of like an echo chamber.

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:07.120
impatience.

00:17:07.120 --> 00:17:11.024
Since you don't get feedback, you get
impatient, and then of course,

00:17:11.024 --> 00:17:15.184
you have the smart people becoming silent,
the incompetent are the only ones

00:17:15.184 --> 00:17:17.744
you rely on, and so when you put the three
of them

00:17:17.744 --> 00:17:22.810
together, you get a trifecta, and you have
a spectacular kind of clusterfug.

00:17:22.810 --> 00:17:26.236
And you can quickly see, actually, many
organizations, teams

00:17:26.236 --> 00:17:29.610
actually taking the off-ramp to a
clusterfug pretty quickly.

00:17:31.310 --> 00:17:33.110
Now, you know, what do you do?

00:17:33.110 --> 00:17:35.770
So, I love reading history.

00:17:35.770 --> 00:17:37.929
The Greek historian Herodotus says.

00:17:39.440 --> 00:17:42.850
The Persians had an interesting decision
protocol to prevent

00:17:42.850 --> 00:17:46.260
illusion, impatience, and incompetence,
and apparently what they would do

00:17:46.260 --> 00:17:49.590
is, they would debate an issue drunk one
day and

00:17:49.590 --> 00:17:52.135
the next day, they would re-debate the
same issue sober.

00:17:52.135 --> 00:17:58.700
[LAUGH] Now, you might ask, what is the
value of such a decision protocol, and,

00:17:58.700 --> 00:18:02.409
put simply, when people are drunk, they
put a lot of information on the table.

00:18:04.230 --> 00:18:07.215
[LAUGH] but their ability to derive a
conclusion is debatable.

00:18:07.215 --> 00:18:09.310
[LAUGH]

00:18:09.310 --> 00:18:14.910
But, on the other hand if you're sober
people are strategic.

00:18:14.910 --> 00:18:17.520
So they don't put a lot of information on
the table.

00:18:17.520 --> 00:18:19.690
However even from the thinnest [UNKNOWN].

00:18:19.690 --> 00:18:23.010
You can certainly construct an influence
if you will.

00:18:23.010 --> 00:18:25.780
Now, our recommendation is not that you
follow what the Persians did.

00:18:25.780 --> 00:18:29.853
Which is, you know, debate an issue drunk
and, you know,

00:18:29.853 --> 00:18:33.155
then rebate it sober the next day, one of
the things we

00:18:33.155 --> 00:18:36.910
started to talk about in the book is To
actually rely

00:18:36.910 --> 00:18:41.410
on something called a pre-mortem, which is
the opposite of a post-mortem.

00:18:41.410 --> 00:18:43.210
So, for instance, if the six of you were
in the

00:18:43.210 --> 00:18:47.870
company and you were thinking of, like,
expansion, or IPU, or whatever

00:18:47.870 --> 00:18:52.490
the decision outcome, I would randomly
assign three people to the failure

00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:56.790
condition and say, imagine the event has
occurred, engage in time travel.

00:18:58.290 --> 00:19:01.200
And then write a story of how that failure
happened.

00:19:02.720 --> 00:19:04.290
Write it just in one page.

00:19:04.290 --> 00:19:07.777
Remember it has to be a story not a list,
and the three people have

00:19:07.777 --> 00:19:10.427
to write that anonymously and
independently cuz

00:19:10.427 --> 00:19:14.390
if you, they collaborate, you'll have
correlated error.

00:19:14.390 --> 00:19:18.310
So on the other side, same thing, you give
them, of course,.

00:19:18.310 --> 00:19:19.760
The scenario of success.

00:19:19.760 --> 00:19:21.180
Imagine the thing has succeeded.

00:19:22.880 --> 00:19:24.560
Write a story of how that succeeded.

00:19:25.770 --> 00:19:29.490
And, what is amazing is, when you get
these stories.

00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:32.150
It is incredible what [INAUDIBLE] you
know?

00:19:32.150 --> 00:19:34.190
When you write, when people write a story.

00:19:34.190 --> 00:19:38.010
What they're doing is, they're actually
giving you, if you will.

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:40.420
You know, a state transition model.

00:19:40.420 --> 00:19:43.302
You have various transition points, you
actually

00:19:43.302 --> 00:19:45.647
can see the downward spiral of a debacle,

00:19:45.647 --> 00:19:51.010
you can see the upward spiral of success,
and then you can quickly sort of debug.

00:19:51.010 --> 00:19:53.909
By way of example let me illustrate, I
just recently did

00:19:53.909 --> 00:19:58.020
a version of this with the leadership of
the Stanford Medical School.

00:19:58.020 --> 00:20:00.830
They really wanna go big guns, create
something called.

00:20:00.830 --> 00:20:04.830
Stanford medicine, where basic science has
to actually work with

00:20:07.140 --> 00:20:08.430
the clinical hospitals.

00:20:08.430 --> 00:20:10.650
And then, of course, the clinical
investigators

00:20:10.650 --> 00:20:12.380
and you gotta work with the hospitals.

00:20:12.380 --> 00:20:14.540
It's amazing the stories of failure people
wrote.

00:20:16.380 --> 00:20:16.880
You know?

00:20:18.250 --> 00:20:21.840
One person knew about side-effects, they
actually sent it as

00:20:21.840 --> 00:20:25.910
a PDF file, but their electronic patient
records don't accept PDFs.

00:20:27.560 --> 00:20:28.630
So the person who knew about

00:20:28.630 --> 00:20:30.830
the side-effects, sadly told this to
everybody.

00:20:30.830 --> 00:20:31.980
But nobody knew that.

00:20:31.980 --> 00:20:34.477
Another person said, "Oh my God, we don't
have nurses to run these trials."

00:20:34.477 --> 00:20:35.150
.

00:20:35.150 --> 00:20:37.430
Another person would say wait a minute.

00:20:37.430 --> 00:20:42.870
You know, we the hospital is taking care
of somebody in a trial.

00:20:42.870 --> 00:20:44.700
This person doesn't know the basic signs.

00:20:44.700 --> 00:20:46.290
They've misdiagnosed the cause of failure.

00:20:46.290 --> 00:20:48.510
You have an action of a lawsuit.

00:20:48.510 --> 00:20:50.932
So things like this when they were
actually put on the

00:20:50.932 --> 00:20:54.360
table the very smart people, of course, in
the medical school faculty.

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:58.451
They quickly said, "Oh my God, we've
really got to get this going."

00:20:58.451 --> 00:21:02.342
And very quickly they actually thought of
lots of possibilities

00:21:02.342 --> 00:21:05.230
and began to put a lot of checks in
measure.

00:21:05.230 --> 00:21:07.285
So just an example, so, think of.

00:21:07.285 --> 00:21:12.470
Premortems if you will, the contemporary
equivalent to what the Persians kinda do.

00:21:12.470 --> 00:21:13.920
So, what's the core message?

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:17.160
Why is it people take the off-ramp to a
clusterfuck?

00:21:17.160 --> 00:21:20.450
And the answer is, usually when you ask
people what is

00:21:20.450 --> 00:21:24.320
scaling, they invariably think of it in
terms of footprint-related metrics.

00:21:25.840 --> 00:21:27.670
And, you know, it's perfectly sensible.

00:21:27.670 --> 00:21:28.420
They're very important.

00:21:29.460 --> 00:21:30.680
But the catch is.

00:21:30.680 --> 00:21:35.980
If you ask people what's desirable social
psychology tells us

00:21:35.980 --> 00:21:39.470
they actually think about this in a very
abstract way.

00:21:39.470 --> 00:21:41.290
That's why they love the metrics.

00:21:41.290 --> 00:21:43.840
You know, so many customers, so many this,
so many that, et cetera.

00:21:43.840 --> 00:21:46.410
They're not really thinking about the
mindset.

00:21:47.690 --> 00:21:52.510
The mindset is actually what allows the
footprint to survive and thrive.

00:21:54.750 --> 00:21:58.330
One example, people really thinking about
the mindset.

00:21:58.330 --> 00:22:01.487
We recently had founders of a company
called [UNKNOWN] they do

00:22:01.487 --> 00:22:05.060
mapping software,they got acquired by
Google for a couple of billion.

00:22:05.060 --> 00:22:08.605
And he was actually in class and he was
giving a fascinating example

00:22:08.605 --> 00:22:10.693
of focusing really on the mindset, and

00:22:10.693 --> 00:22:13.650
[INAUDIBLE] a number of other important
truths.

00:22:13.650 --> 00:22:16.933
So they get 30 million you know, you,in an
earlier phase of the

00:22:16.933 --> 00:22:19.262
revolution, they get 30 million dollars,

00:22:19.262 --> 00:22:21.649
and the [UNKNOWN] are telling them,
"scale!"

00:22:21.649 --> 00:22:23.820
you got 100 people, hire 400 more.

00:22:25.940 --> 00:22:28.130
And the employees also expect head count
to go

00:22:28.130 --> 00:22:30.630
up, the company to grow, you know, all
these things.

00:22:30.630 --> 00:22:34.770
And the founders start to think about it,
and they thought about it, and then after.

00:22:34.770 --> 00:22:36.520
They made a decision and then they called
all the

00:22:36.520 --> 00:22:40.620
employees of the firm, and they announced
a hiring freeze.

00:22:42.150 --> 00:22:43.460
People were stunned.

00:22:43.460 --> 00:22:45.500
The employees said, are you out of your

00:22:45.500 --> 00:22:47.550
mind, we just got 30 million dollars,
they're telling

00:22:47.550 --> 00:22:52.170
you to scale, you know, is this a vote of
no confidence in the future of the firm?

00:22:52.170 --> 00:22:54.560
Why a hiring freeze now?

00:22:54.560 --> 00:22:56.260
It seems insane.

00:22:56.260 --> 00:23:00.070
And the founders stood up and said look,
we had 100 people

00:23:00.070 --> 00:23:05.240
we're gonna hire 400 smart people, all of
them have great ideas.

00:23:05.240 --> 00:23:07.760
The problem is when you hire 400 smart
people and

00:23:07.760 --> 00:23:10.310
they come to you, I have to say no 400
times.

00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:14.660
Because we have to focus.

00:23:14.660 --> 00:23:18.680
We can't just kind of get everybody's idea
into the mix, I've got to say no.

00:23:18.680 --> 00:23:22.910
And I don't want to create 400 demotivated
people in one fell swoop.

00:23:22.910 --> 00:23:25.050
And he said, the smart people that we

00:23:25.050 --> 00:23:28.230
hire, they're smart, but they're also over
confident.

00:23:28.230 --> 00:23:30.470
They think they can hit the ground running
on day one.

00:23:30.470 --> 00:23:31.530
They can't.

00:23:31.530 --> 00:23:34.120
They need to take three weeks to get
ready.

00:23:34.120 --> 00:23:37.770
If I hire 400 people in one step, I've

00:23:37.770 --> 00:23:42.000
actually altered dramatically the ratio of
unproductive to productive people.

00:23:43.460 --> 00:23:44.920
And then what am I going to do as a CEO?

00:23:44.920 --> 00:23:46.515
I'm going to spend time thinking about

00:23:46.515 --> 00:23:48.890
how to make the produ, unproductive people
productive.

00:23:48.890 --> 00:23:49.559
And I don't want to do that.

00:23:50.640 --> 00:23:53.660
The real number we need to move is one
metric,

00:23:53.660 --> 00:23:57.730
and that is, the three month user re user
retention metric.

00:23:58.740 --> 00:24:02.264
We have users, there's a lot of churn, and
the real test of our

00:24:02.264 --> 00:24:06.630
ability is, how many of them do we retain
over a three month period?

00:24:06.630 --> 00:24:09.220
And so, one of the employees then said,
well, what's my job?

00:24:10.370 --> 00:24:12.230
And you know he said, well, your job is
simple.

00:24:12.230 --> 00:24:14.760
In fact, the job of everyone of us, is
very simple.

00:24:14.760 --> 00:24:19.761
Everyday, we've got to ask, what did I do
today, to move that number?

00:24:19.761 --> 00:24:21.950
Think about it.

00:24:21.950 --> 00:24:25.635
And this story is also particularly
resonant with me, because

00:24:25.635 --> 00:24:29.810
what it sorta [INAUDIBLE] to you is,
scaling is actually about.

00:24:29.810 --> 00:24:32.320
When you scale an organization, you really
got to figure

00:24:32.320 --> 00:24:37.120
out that it not just mindless addition,
it's actually smart subtraction.

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:39.330
In fact, when you're in the business of
scaling

00:24:39.330 --> 00:24:41.610
excellence, you really are like a race car
driver.

00:24:41.610 --> 00:24:44.530
You've got to figure out when to
accelerate but,

00:24:44.530 --> 00:24:47.510
also, when to break and when to change the
gears.

00:24:47.510 --> 00:24:49.210
And that's what they figured out.

00:24:49.210 --> 00:24:52.670
And of course the company succeeded
subsequently.

00:24:52.670 --> 00:24:56.906
And you quickly realize if people
mindlessly and individuals,

00:24:56.906 --> 00:25:00.599
you're definitely taking the on ramp to a
clusterfug.

00:25:00.599 --> 00:25:04.200
So, you know, I'm going to sorta of move
on.

00:25:04.200 --> 00:25:08.310
And the problem of course with scaling is
if you don't do this well it's dilution.

00:25:08.310 --> 00:25:12.929
Here's a confessional, a little memo from
Howard Schultz of Starbucks saying as we

00:25:12.929 --> 00:25:18.420
move from 13 hundred to 13 thousand, we of
course lost sight of the customer.

00:25:18.420 --> 00:25:21.410
And the constant is, in all the excellent
organizations we

00:25:21.410 --> 00:25:26.070
visited, the executives invariably see the
glass as half empty.

00:25:26.070 --> 00:25:27.780
And nowhere did we find somebody who sorta

00:25:27.780 --> 00:25:31.420
says you know, we're doing great and you
know.

00:25:31.420 --> 00:25:33.910
you, you just don't find that at all, they
all assume, my God, we

00:25:33.910 --> 00:25:36.130
gotta do all these things all the

00:25:36.130 --> 00:25:39.260
time, and that's kind of that relentless
restlessness.

00:25:39.260 --> 00:25:42.213
So, I'm gonna give you a couple of big
lessons, maybe a choice

00:25:42.213 --> 00:25:45.350
point or two, and a quick overview of some
of the main principles.

00:25:45.350 --> 00:25:48.510
We, obviously won't be able to dive in too
deep, because

00:25:48.510 --> 00:25:51.660
we, we, we end at 6:15, and I wanna make
sure.

00:25:51.660 --> 00:25:53.350
There's some time for Q and A.

00:25:53.350 --> 00:25:58.690
So one of the big lessons is scaling up
excellence isn't just an air war.

00:25:58.690 --> 00:26:00.740
It's actually a ground war.

00:26:00.740 --> 00:26:02.400
So what are the characteristics of an air
war?

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:05.108
When you think of an air war what do you
think about?

00:26:05.108 --> 00:26:06.892
What's that?

00:26:06.892 --> 00:26:09.830
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; High level.

00:26:09.830 --> 00:26:10.450
Abstract.

00:26:10.450 --> 00:26:11.280
No details.

00:26:11.280 --> 00:26:12.010
Absolutely.

00:26:12.010 --> 00:26:12.510
What else?

00:26:13.720 --> 00:26:15.140
Sorry, top down absolutely.

00:26:15.140 --> 00:26:16.113
What else?

00:26:16.113 --> 00:26:16.906
Huh?

00:26:16.906 --> 00:26:17.557
&gt;&gt; collateral damage.

00:26:17.557 --> 00:26:18.640
&gt;&gt; Collateral damage.

00:26:18.640 --> 00:26:21.469
&gt;&gt; Uh,indeed collateral damage can
actually be

00:26:21.469 --> 00:26:24.587
very high there's a fascinating historic
account

00:26:24.587 --> 00:26:28.877
of the British bombing of Germany and they
found out that what is it?

00:26:28.877 --> 00:26:31.670
[LAUGH] Twenty percent of the ordnance
landed

00:26:31.670 --> 00:26:34.395
within five miles of the intended target.

00:26:34.395 --> 00:26:38.210
[LAUGH] Yeah, the rest of it was like all
over the place.

00:26:38.210 --> 00:26:40.530
That tells you something about collateral
damage.

00:26:40.530 --> 00:26:41.510
What about a ground war?

00:26:41.510 --> 00:26:44.323
Were [CROSSTALK] very personal?

00:26:44.323 --> 00:26:44.936
What else?

00:26:44.936 --> 00:26:46.631
&gt;&gt; More intense?

00:26:46.631 --> 00:26:48.326
&gt;&gt; More intense.

00:26:48.326 --> 00:26:49.499
&gt;&gt; Actual engagement.

00:26:49.499 --> 00:26:50.680
&gt;&gt; Actual engagement.

00:26:50.680 --> 00:26:56.150
So, when we go to companies, you can
quickly find out who is thinking about.

00:26:56.150 --> 00:27:00.490
And in thinking in just an air war is not
enough.

00:27:00.490 --> 00:27:03.270
I'm not saying air wars are bad obviously
you need a little bit of both.

00:27:03.270 --> 00:27:07.410
But what you want to think about is you
really want to focus on the ground war.

00:27:07.410 --> 00:27:09.930
And the reason is the following, because
remember when

00:27:09.930 --> 00:27:13.100
you think air war, you're only thinking
very abstractly.

00:27:13.100 --> 00:27:16.070
When you thinking ground war you're
thinking very, very concretely.

00:27:17.850 --> 00:27:22.470
So for us the compelling distinction we
found is when I go to companies I ask

00:27:22.470 --> 00:27:25.820
people hey how do you think you need to
get all these people to do something?

00:27:25.820 --> 00:27:27.000
And the people who are in an air

00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:30.960
war mode instantly leap to incentive based
reasoning.

00:27:30.960 --> 00:27:33.490
We just need to figure out how to reward

00:27:33.490 --> 00:27:35.310
them if they do or punish them if they
don't.

00:27:35.310 --> 00:27:37.420
That's is that's all we need to do.

00:27:37.420 --> 00:27:40.370
But the people who think of it in a ground
war term, they

00:27:40.370 --> 00:27:42.010
actually sort of think, you know,
motivation

00:27:42.010 --> 00:27:43.760
isn't the problem, tools are the problem.

00:27:45.090 --> 00:27:48.890
The question is what kinda tools can we
give these people to make them succeed.

00:27:48.890 --> 00:27:50.590
Very, very different.

00:27:50.590 --> 00:27:53.300
So, I'll give you a little illustration of

00:27:53.300 --> 00:27:56.440
how powerfully somebody thought of a
ground war literally.

00:27:56.440 --> 00:28:00.380
In the context of a war and that was a
general called Matthew Ridgeway.

00:28:00.380 --> 00:28:02.850
Now this was actually in the Korean War,
which is

00:28:02.850 --> 00:28:07.250
for some strange reason not as you know,
given the kind

00:28:07.250 --> 00:28:10.050
of attention, because some of the most
brilliant acts of

00:28:10.050 --> 00:28:14.350
leadership really in the 20th century
occurred, in the, Korean War.

00:28:14.350 --> 00:28:15.694
So imagine.

00:28:15.694 --> 00:28:20.163
You know, [UNKNOWN] is there in Tokyo and
you know, he's

00:28:20.163 --> 00:28:25.390
consumed by sensible delusion and delusion
and then Truman fires him.

00:28:26.700 --> 00:28:28.190
And you're Ridgeway.

00:28:28.190 --> 00:28:28.930
You're 42 years old.

00:28:28.930 --> 00:28:30.670
You've never fought in Korea.

00:28:30.670 --> 00:28:32.070
You're in charge of like 500,000 men.

00:28:33.290 --> 00:28:37.914
Who simply being beaten, bruised,
battered,

00:28:37.914 --> 00:28:41.630
basically by the Chinese first army.

00:28:41.630 --> 00:28:43.390
You can't go and get YouTube's.

00:28:43.390 --> 00:28:45.290
You can't give people a speech and say
jeez let's

00:28:45.290 --> 00:28:48.140
kinda go and do something, you can't do
all that.

00:28:48.140 --> 00:28:49.960
But look at what Ridgeway does.

00:28:49.960 --> 00:28:53.545
The first thing he does is, he actually,
you know, he says.

00:28:53.545 --> 00:28:56.570
I, I need to understand the topography, of
Korea.

00:28:56.570 --> 00:28:58.690
So he goes to on a bomber and there's a

00:28:58.690 --> 00:29:00.940
navigator and he tells the navigator can I
take your seat?

00:29:00.940 --> 00:29:03.040
I want to see what the topography

00:29:05.080 --> 00:29:05.140
is.

00:29:05.140 --> 00:29:08.866
And he realizes oh my God, you know, lots
of hills and ridges and rivers and then he

00:29:08.866 --> 00:29:11.242
meets these regimental commanders and he
says do you

00:29:11.242 --> 00:29:13.890
know the name of the river close by to
you?

00:29:13.890 --> 00:29:15.440
I said, do you know how the deep the water
is?

00:29:15.440 --> 00:29:15.640
No.

00:29:15.640 --> 00:29:20.550
You're fired, because you're putting
people in harm's way, you have no idea.

00:29:20.550 --> 00:29:23.410
Apparently what the Pentagon had done is,
there were a lot of people

00:29:23.410 --> 00:29:24.890
in the Pentagon who apparently weren't

00:29:24.890 --> 00:29:27.890
given leadership opportunities in World
War Two.

00:29:27.890 --> 00:29:30.180
They moved a lot of files and somebody
thought, you know.

00:29:30.180 --> 00:29:31.690
We really need to kind of give them
leadership

00:29:31.690 --> 00:29:33.970
opportunities so let's send them to Korea
as regimental commanders.

00:29:35.240 --> 00:29:38.280
When the heaviest thing you've moved if a
file between two desks,

00:29:38.280 --> 00:29:41.520
it's not exactly the right kind of person
to send to Korea.

00:29:41.520 --> 00:29:45.149
And so the things that he sorta does to

00:29:45.149 --> 00:29:49.938
give you a sense, no speeches, none of
these things.

00:29:49.938 --> 00:29:56.046
But when he travels, no fighter escort,
most shot at General, in the 20th Century.

00:29:56.046 --> 00:29:57.895
He says, my men are getting shot.

00:29:57.895 --> 00:29:59.995
Why do I need a fighter escort?

00:29:59.995 --> 00:30:04.270
Every time he goes and visits troops, it's
the cold Korean winter.

00:30:04.270 --> 00:30:07.750
The only thing he has in his Jeep, ample
supply of gloves.

00:30:08.920 --> 00:30:11.462
He gives gloves to soldiers and say, you
can hold your carbine.

00:30:11.462 --> 00:30:15.070
So he goes to a forward operating post.

00:30:15.070 --> 00:30:15.830
And he looks at a map.

00:30:16.850 --> 00:30:21.710
And, you know, blue pins US, and red
communist forces.

00:30:21.710 --> 00:30:23.080
So if you're Ridgeway.

00:30:23.080 --> 00:30:24.720
You got 20 men in the room.

00:30:24.720 --> 00:30:26.400
What's the question you're going to ask?

00:30:26.400 --> 00:30:30.520
What's the diagnostic question you're
going to ask?

00:30:30.520 --> 00:30:31.510
Imagine.

00:30:31.510 --> 00:30:31.990
It's clear.

00:30:31.990 --> 00:30:35.020
You know where the US forces are, where
the Communist forces are.

00:30:35.020 --> 00:30:35.980
There are 20 men sitting there.

00:30:35.980 --> 00:30:36.850
You're the general.

00:30:36.850 --> 00:30:39.310
What's the, if you had one question to ask
to

00:30:39.310 --> 00:30:42.840
diagnose what is going on, what would that
question be?

00:30:42.840 --> 00:30:43.340
Hm?

00:30:44.720 --> 00:30:45.658
Come on, somebody take a guess.

00:30:45.658 --> 00:30:47.181
Come on.

00:30:47.181 --> 00:30:51.680
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; What do you need, that's good.

00:30:51.680 --> 00:30:52.870
Which direction, that's good.

00:30:52.870 --> 00:30:53.370
What else?

00:30:54.410 --> 00:30:54.910
Wind.

00:30:56.600 --> 00:30:57.500
How many people?

00:30:57.500 --> 00:31:01.310
They're all very legitimate questions, but
the question he asks is.

00:31:01.310 --> 00:31:05.640
How old is the information on which the
pen positions are based on?

00:31:07.510 --> 00:31:10.330
And the men look at him and say, they're
15 days old.

00:31:10.330 --> 00:31:11.010
What does that tell you?

00:31:12.040 --> 00:31:13.610
Obviously, it's obsolete.

00:31:13.610 --> 00:31:15.380
But it also tells you the men are afraid.

00:31:15.380 --> 00:31:18.260
They don't want to go on a foot patrol.

00:31:18.260 --> 00:31:19.960
Because they're afraid of getting
ambushed.

00:31:19.960 --> 00:31:22.630
That's the reason you're not getting high
quality information.

00:31:22.630 --> 00:31:24.680
And, that's when he tells people, look.

00:31:24.680 --> 00:31:27.341
I need information that's three hours
fresh, we're going to

00:31:27.341 --> 00:31:30.510
support you in this way, we're going to do
this, etcetera.

00:31:30.510 --> 00:31:34.097
And, it's these same people who were
refusing to go, as

00:31:34.097 --> 00:31:37.880
it were, on a foot patrol, the very same
people push.

00:31:37.880 --> 00:31:41.420
The Chinese and not going in Communist
forces to the 36th Parallel.

00:31:41.420 --> 00:31:44.786
There's actually a lovely story of a crazy
Marine General

00:31:44.786 --> 00:31:47.809
whose unit is surrounded on all sides and
the men

00:31:47.809 --> 00:31:50.418
tell him, the enemy is in front of us,
behind

00:31:50.418 --> 00:31:53.610
us, to the left, to the right, what do we
do?

00:31:53.610 --> 00:31:58.100
And this guy is slightly maniacal, so he
says, wonderful.

00:31:58.100 --> 00:31:59.615
They've got nowhere to run.

00:31:59.615 --> 00:32:03.560
[LAUGH]
You've gotta be crazy to think like that.

00:32:03.560 --> 00:32:05.640
They actually punched through the
corridor.

00:32:05.640 --> 00:32:08.221
So here's you know, this is what one

00:32:08.221 --> 00:32:11.272
McKinsey consultant told us, the real
thing about the

00:32:11.272 --> 00:32:13.858
ground war is, you gotta get a thousand
people

00:32:13.858 --> 00:32:16.560
to move one foot forward at the same time.

00:32:16.560 --> 00:32:20.440
In an air war, obviously, one person can
go a thousand feet forward.

00:32:20.440 --> 00:32:25.320
So, the second thing is, scaling is a lot
about felt accountability.

00:32:25.320 --> 00:32:29.120
Everybody talks of people feeling a sense
of ownership, and that's true.

00:32:29.120 --> 00:32:31.800
You certainly need to feel you own the
place.

00:32:31.800 --> 00:32:34.990
But if that's the only thing you do, that
can be dangerous.

00:32:34.990 --> 00:32:37.360
You can quickly begin to think of
yourself.

00:32:37.360 --> 00:32:40.200
It can get very swiftly egocentric.

00:32:40.200 --> 00:32:42.000
And so what we find out about felt

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:45.130
accountability is you really need not only
the

00:32:45.130 --> 00:32:49.420
feeling that you own the place, but you
also need the feeling the place owns you.

00:32:50.900 --> 00:32:52.600
So you need actually the pull of

00:32:52.600 --> 00:32:55.650
obligation, and then the push of
committment.

00:32:55.650 --> 00:32:57.500
Both of those are needed.

00:32:57.500 --> 00:33:01.980
And in the, one of the SCPs, there are a
couple of SCPers sitting here as well.

00:33:01.980 --> 00:33:04.850
I was actually talking about this
interesting case study.

00:33:04.850 --> 00:33:06.950
Anybody remembers this?

00:33:06.950 --> 00:33:09.760
What's the hotel on the, what's the
building on the left?

00:33:09.760 --> 00:33:10.830
I gave the game away by.

00:33:11.900 --> 00:33:12.740
The Taj, exactly.

00:33:12.740 --> 00:33:16.130
November 26 you had terrorists attacking
the hotel.

00:33:16.130 --> 00:33:17.440
And, you know?

00:33:17.440 --> 00:33:20.930
You had employees like these young ladies
on the left, doing things.

00:33:22.500 --> 00:33:24.660
And, what's amazing is, you know?

00:33:24.660 --> 00:33:29.170
When you have a terrorist attack, what do
you expect employees to do?

00:33:29.170 --> 00:33:32.320
Head for the exit, cuz they know where the
exits are.

00:33:32.320 --> 00:33:34.824
And that day there was no command and
control, the general

00:33:34.824 --> 00:33:38.150
manager is rushing in from somewhere, can
only get to the lobby.

00:33:38.150 --> 00:33:43.150
His family is being consumed by fire in
the 35th or the 36th floor.

00:33:43.150 --> 00:33:46.340
Nobody's giving orders, nothing,
everything is in disarray.

00:33:46.340 --> 00:33:49.002
Well, what was amazing is young ladies
like this who

00:33:49.002 --> 00:33:53.360
were actually overseeing the reception for
the outgoing CEO of Unilever.

00:33:53.360 --> 00:33:56.570
You know, in a conference room quickly
barricaded the room, made

00:33:56.570 --> 00:34:00.590
sure that people were hydrated, the wives
were separated from the husbands.

00:34:00.590 --> 00:34:02.910
Cooks escorted people down the stairs.

00:34:02.910 --> 00:34:04.740
A lot of cooks lost their lives.

00:34:04.740 --> 00:34:07.070
Because they gave their bodies as shields.

00:34:07.070 --> 00:34:10.690
And later, when they asked some of these
employees, you guys were heroic.

00:34:10.690 --> 00:34:14.590
They said no, we actually were doing our
job.

00:34:14.590 --> 00:34:15.940
What's their job?

00:34:15.940 --> 00:34:19.510
Their job actually is not to be an
ambassador of the Taj

00:34:19.510 --> 00:34:21.860
to a customer, but to actually be an
advocate of the customer.

00:34:23.200 --> 00:34:25.720
The Taj actually hires very carefully,
though.

00:34:25.720 --> 00:34:28.690
You obviously can't do that in the United
States for legal reasons.

00:34:28.690 --> 00:34:30.459
But one of the things they do is the never

00:34:30.459 --> 00:34:33.630
hire people who are usually from the major
cities of India.

00:34:33.630 --> 00:34:36.570
They always hire people who are from the
smaller cities of India.

00:34:36.570 --> 00:34:40.540
Because the tradition of [INAUDIBLE] being
god is actually quite vibrant.

00:34:40.540 --> 00:34:42.803
In the bigger cities, modernization has
actually

00:34:42.803 --> 00:34:45.070
kind of assaulted all of those norms.

00:34:45.070 --> 00:34:47.070
They do a whole bunch of other things.

00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:49.580
As I was in this SEP class, talking about,

00:34:49.580 --> 00:34:51.820
there's one of these executives made a
very sensible point.

00:34:51.820 --> 00:34:55.480
And he said, Professor Robert, surely this
is a case of crisis.

00:34:55.480 --> 00:34:59.210
You obviously expect your employees to
rally around this.

00:34:59.210 --> 00:35:03.020
You know, isn't the real test what the Taj
does every day?

00:35:04.350 --> 00:35:06.110
And I was about to say something.

00:35:06.110 --> 00:35:08.364
We actually had another participant who
was a

00:35:08.364 --> 00:35:11.420
senior executive at [INAUDIBLE] guy called
John Thomas.

00:35:11.420 --> 00:35:13.940
He said, let me tell you about my
experience.

00:35:13.940 --> 00:35:17.590
He said, I flew with my wife and three
year old to India.

00:35:18.678 --> 00:35:22.108
Jet lag tired, the kid's crying, we're all
ornery,

00:35:22.108 --> 00:35:24.056
the Taj sends a car to pick us up, on

00:35:24.056 --> 00:35:26.947
route the baby is crying and I'm thinking,
oh, my

00:35:26.947 --> 00:35:31.370
God, we gotta go through checking,
check-in delay and everything.

00:35:31.370 --> 00:35:33.082
They come to the Taj and somebody meets

00:35:33.082 --> 00:35:35.340
them and says, you've already been checked
in.

00:35:35.340 --> 00:35:36.740
They take them to the room.

00:35:36.740 --> 00:35:39.610
They open the doors and you know there's a
little flask

00:35:39.610 --> 00:35:42.470
of milk and a bunch of cookies for the
three year old.

00:35:42.470 --> 00:35:45.000
So John looks at them and says, how did
you know?

00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:48.070
And the guy says, when you got in the car
your baby was

00:35:48.070 --> 00:35:51.710
crying, the driver called the front desk
and said, baby on board, jet lagged.

00:35:53.310 --> 00:35:54.045
Expedite.

00:35:54.045 --> 00:35:55.590
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:35:55.590 --> 00:35:59.790
&gt;&gt; Now isn't that an amazing case of
accountability, felt accountability?

00:35:59.790 --> 00:36:01.370
That's what felt accountability is.

00:36:01.370 --> 00:36:03.200
And by the way, the driver isn't an

00:36:03.200 --> 00:36:06.500
employee of the Taj, but a contractual
individual.

00:36:06.500 --> 00:36:08.420
So here's this one woman called Fifi.

00:36:08.420 --> 00:36:11.110
And when we did a study of Wyeth.

00:36:11.110 --> 00:36:14.910
You know the one of the Wyatt plants, what
did they do?

00:36:14.910 --> 00:36:16.590
They actually thought of

00:36:18.880 --> 00:36:21.240
equipment changes as breaks.

00:36:21.240 --> 00:36:25.270
So, mentally, when you categorize
something as a break, you're kind of

00:36:25.270 --> 00:36:28.410
like, loose and easy, and you don't,
there's no sense of urgency.

00:36:28.410 --> 00:36:30.370
But these people sort of said "wait a
minute, it's not a

00:36:30.370 --> 00:36:34.000
break" that we've got organize ourselves
as though it's a pit stop.

00:36:34.000 --> 00:36:36.904
Just that person and her team members and
look at

00:36:36.904 --> 00:36:39.733
sort of what they did, in terms of, you
know,

00:36:39.733 --> 00:36:43.147
not just the number of syringe production,
but how they

00:36:43.147 --> 00:36:46.510
cut down the changeover time and the like
and so forth.

00:36:46.510 --> 00:36:49.130
He's a quick example of a United Pilot.

00:36:49.130 --> 00:36:51.230
Saying I used to do all these things.

00:36:51.230 --> 00:36:52.480
And now, what do I do?

00:36:52.480 --> 00:36:53.740
I gave up.

00:36:53.740 --> 00:36:56.430
And I'm an Eagle Scout, and I'm an
entrepreneur.

00:36:56.430 --> 00:37:00.640
So I'm gonna be very quick here, and talk
about a couple of ideas.

00:37:00.640 --> 00:37:04.380
So, the most important choice point is
when you scale.

00:37:04.380 --> 00:37:06.960
Do you want more, or do you want them to
be better?

00:37:06.960 --> 00:37:09.330
So that's kind of one important choice
point.

00:37:09.330 --> 00:37:11.362
The second choice point is, and this is
the

00:37:11.362 --> 00:37:13.720
one I want to spend a little more time on.

00:37:13.720 --> 00:37:16.680
Do you want to be a Catholic, or do you
want to be a Buddhist?

00:37:16.680 --> 00:37:18.900
I went to Jesuit school in India.

00:37:18.900 --> 00:37:22.660
I actually meditated and extracted from
St. Francis of Asisi every morning.

00:37:22.660 --> 00:37:24.840
So, you know, even though I was born a
Hindu.

00:37:24.840 --> 00:37:28.350
So, you know, certainly Jesuit education
has an enormous imprint on you.

00:37:29.960 --> 00:37:32.900
But what do we mean by Catholic versus
Buddhist?

00:37:32.900 --> 00:37:35.750
See, if you're a Catholic, it's like
you're, like In-N-Out Burger.

00:37:35.750 --> 00:37:40.160
You've got a specific design or like
Intel's Copy Exactly model.

00:37:41.230 --> 00:37:45.080
But, if you're a Buddhist, what you do is,
you don't allow a thousand flowers

00:37:45.080 --> 00:37:47.730
to bloom but you give them good guard

00:37:47.730 --> 00:37:51.480
rails, in the form of principles or
protocols.

00:37:51.480 --> 00:37:54.080
When you can actually allow for local
realization.

00:37:55.090 --> 00:37:58.470
And one of the heroes in our book is Chip
Connelly, who

00:37:58.470 --> 00:38:03.650
actually just has joined Arab B and B as
their customer relations person.

00:38:03.650 --> 00:38:06.460
He used to own a chain of hotels called
Joie de Vivre.

00:38:06.460 --> 00:38:07.060
That he sold.

00:38:07.060 --> 00:38:10.170
And then all over the place, like the
Phoenix Hotel to the Rex

00:38:10.170 --> 00:38:15.010
Hotel, and their range in price from 100
bucks a night to 700 bucks.

00:38:15.010 --> 00:38:16.790
So when we are asked, Chip, how do we

00:38:16.790 --> 00:38:20.690
go about building these hotels, look at
his Buddhist protocol.

00:38:20.690 --> 00:38:24.368
He asks his employees, any time you come
to me with a request

00:38:24.368 --> 00:38:29.170
for funds to start a new hotel, you need
to answer four questions.

00:38:29.170 --> 00:38:33.350
Question one: What magazine does your
customer read?

00:38:33.350 --> 00:38:35.930
If it's The New Yorker, I know exactly who
they are.

00:38:35.930 --> 00:38:37.900
You don't need to tell me anything else.

00:38:37.900 --> 00:38:43.620
Question two: What are the five adjectives
you would use to describe the experience?

00:38:43.620 --> 00:38:44.840
So, if it's The New Yorker, is

00:38:44.840 --> 00:38:47.720
sophisticated one of the adjectives, for
example?

00:38:47.720 --> 00:38:50.731
If that's indeed an adjective, how will
the customer

00:38:50.731 --> 00:38:54.370
quickly sense that through his or her five
senses?

00:38:54.370 --> 00:38:57.180
Nobody needs to tell the person this is a
sophisticated place.

00:38:57.180 --> 00:38:59.400
You walk in, you instantly know, you know?

00:38:59.400 --> 00:39:02.560
Maybe they're playing a particular kind of
music, through a sense

00:39:02.560 --> 00:39:05.740
of ear, or you see the bar's like a
library, whatever.

00:39:05.740 --> 00:39:07.133
And the next question is, how do

00:39:07.133 --> 00:39:09.410
the employees know this through their five
senses?

00:39:10.570 --> 00:39:13.110
You know, what kind of uniform are you
wearing?

00:39:13.110 --> 00:39:15.430
You know from that onwards.

00:39:15.430 --> 00:39:17.260
So, when he's getting people to look at
how

00:39:17.260 --> 00:39:20.300
detailed he's making people to think
through a problem.

00:39:20.300 --> 00:39:23.800
Seemingly simple protocol, but in every
hotel you actually have

00:39:23.800 --> 00:39:27.620
to come up with different answers to the
same five questions.

00:39:27.620 --> 00:39:31.570
So, I wanna sort of give us a little bit
of time.

00:39:31.570 --> 00:39:32.870
There are a number principles.

00:39:32.870 --> 00:39:35.080
I won't talk about all of them.

00:39:35.080 --> 00:39:37.780
I'm actually gonna talk about a couple of
things.

00:39:37.780 --> 00:39:41.710
The first one is, when you scale, most of

00:39:41.710 --> 00:39:44.520
us think that structure is the way to
coordinate.

00:39:46.950 --> 00:39:48.780
And that certainly is useful.

00:39:48.780 --> 00:39:52.200
But smart leaders very quickly realize

00:39:52.200 --> 00:39:55.350
stories are actually very powerful
coordination devices.

00:39:56.570 --> 00:39:59.578
Sometimes they're a substitute for
structure,

00:39:59.578 --> 00:40:01.869
and other times they're a complement

00:40:01.869 --> 00:40:06.520
to structure, and the kind of so stories
can be very powerful.

00:40:06.520 --> 00:40:10.279
So one lovely experiment done by Laura
Boroditsky, who used to be in the

00:40:10.279 --> 00:40:12.661
psych department here, she and her
graduate

00:40:12.661 --> 00:40:15.620
student Peggy Thebadore did a brilliant
experiment.

00:40:15.620 --> 00:40:19.790
So they took a large group of people like
you, randomly put you into two conditions.

00:40:19.790 --> 00:40:22.285
And gave you one page of information that
was

00:40:22.285 --> 00:40:27.140
completely identical about a fictional
city called Adison in California.

00:40:27.140 --> 00:40:31.480
So they gave you about, information about
the crime rate in California.

00:40:31.480 --> 00:40:33.740
Income, education, all these things.

00:40:33.740 --> 00:40:35.120
There was just one difference.

00:40:36.250 --> 00:40:39.440
People on this side of the room, they were

00:40:39.440 --> 00:40:43.220
told that crime is lurking like a beast in
California.

00:40:44.540 --> 00:40:49.679
And this side of the room, they said crime
is lurking like a virus in Addison City.

00:40:50.850 --> 00:40:53.741
And they ask people, what do you think the
city of Addison oughta do?

00:40:53.741 --> 00:40:56.247
So what do you do to a beast?

00:40:56.247 --> 00:40:57.867
What do you do to a beast?

00:40:57.867 --> 00:40:58.395
Kill it.

00:40:58.395 --> 00:40:58.857
Cage it.

00:40:58.857 --> 00:40:59.395
Slay it.

00:40:59.395 --> 00:41:00.070
All of that.

00:41:00.070 --> 00:41:02.870
And so when you ask people what should the
city of Addison do?

00:41:02.870 --> 00:41:04.730
What, what did they say?

00:41:04.730 --> 00:41:07.310
More cops, more jails, tougher
enforcement.

00:41:07.310 --> 00:41:11.160
And by the way, this is independent of
political persuasion.

00:41:11.160 --> 00:41:15.190
You could be a Democrat, who hates all
this, but the moment you've got this

00:41:15.190 --> 00:41:17.470
like beast framing there in your head,

00:41:17.470 --> 00:41:20.420
you're saying more jails, more this, more
that.

00:41:20.420 --> 00:41:21.670
What do you do to a virus?

00:41:22.980 --> 00:41:23.720
Treat it?

00:41:23.720 --> 00:41:25.130
You immaculate, you cure it.

00:41:25.130 --> 00:41:27.230
So, what should the city of Addison do?

00:41:28.810 --> 00:41:30.976
What the city of Addison ought to do

00:41:30.976 --> 00:41:34.712
is more counseling, more rehabilitation,
and even if you

00:41:34.712 --> 00:41:39.121
were a fervent republican who was against
government expenditures,

00:41:39.121 --> 00:41:41.230
you said we need to do all of this.

00:41:41.230 --> 00:41:44.813
It's amazing, so Ben Leda and her
colleagues said, you know, maybe

00:41:44.813 --> 00:41:48.890
we are using words like praying, what if
just use beast and virus.

00:41:48.890 --> 00:41:50.270
Same result.

00:41:50.270 --> 00:41:53.710
So they would use the word beast or virus
in the first two, three lines.

00:41:53.710 --> 00:41:56.460
And they said, what happens if we do it at
the very end?

00:41:56.460 --> 00:41:57.830
No effect.

00:41:57.830 --> 00:42:01.430
But when you actually have a small word
like that, very quickly what

00:42:01.430 --> 00:42:05.280
the story is doing is, all of us have
mental butlers in our heads.

00:42:06.320 --> 00:42:08.169
And these word cues.

00:42:09.760 --> 00:42:12.429
You instantly initiate the story telling
in your head,

00:42:12.429 --> 00:42:16.020
and they reflect, of course, in your
political preferences.

00:42:16.020 --> 00:42:18.760
Sometimes stories can be even more, I
mean,

00:42:18.760 --> 00:42:21.590
the stories or cause can be even more
dramatic.

00:42:21.590 --> 00:42:24.490
And one of the things we chronicle in our
book is about British

00:42:24.490 --> 00:42:28.800
petroleum where the head of the retail
unit wanted to go after Shell.

00:42:28.800 --> 00:42:30.970
And his storyline, was slam the clam.

00:42:32.790 --> 00:42:33.595
So who's the clam?

00:42:33.595 --> 00:42:34.820
Obvious, Shell.

00:42:34.820 --> 00:42:37.950
So I went to him and I said, Phil, come
on, this sounds kinda hokey.

00:42:37.950 --> 00:42:40.420
I mean, you just say slam the clam and
stuff happens?

00:42:40.420 --> 00:42:43.350
Come on, tell me how, how does stuff
happen here?

00:42:43.350 --> 00:42:46.031
And he said, you know, I give you that,
you might think

00:42:46.031 --> 00:42:47.631
it's a simple little thing, but

00:42:47.631 --> 00:42:50.810
it's extraordinarily how powerful the
effect is.

00:42:50.810 --> 00:42:54.120
First, he said, I didn't need a slide to
get that idea across.

00:42:54.120 --> 00:42:55.550
I just told people, slam the clam.

00:42:55.550 --> 00:42:57.190
They got it, it stuck.

00:42:57.190 --> 00:42:59.800
So it survived the car journey home.

00:42:59.800 --> 00:43:02.406
Most of us listen to leaders who when they
make big announcements, you

00:43:02.406 --> 00:43:06.020
simply sit in your car, you drive home,
you've forgotten what they said.

00:43:06.020 --> 00:43:09.270
So this guy said, look, it survived the
car trip home.

00:43:09.270 --> 00:43:10.520
And I said, okay, I grant you that.

00:43:10.520 --> 00:43:11.930
What else did it do?

00:43:11.930 --> 00:43:13.670
And he said, you know, people would sit in
a meeting,

00:43:13.670 --> 00:43:16.000
and they would say, where should we put
new gas stations?

00:43:16.000 --> 00:43:17.890
Ultimately, it would all come down to one
thing.

00:43:17.890 --> 00:43:21.180
Does it slam the clam?

00:43:21.180 --> 00:43:23.470
Where do we put advertising dollars?

00:43:23.470 --> 00:43:25.390
Does it slam the clam?

00:43:25.390 --> 00:43:29.272
Where do we put R&amp;D dollars, where do we
hire people, anything you did, you

00:43:29.272 --> 00:43:33.720
just had one question to actually focus
on, and that's the power of the story.

00:43:33.720 --> 00:43:35.660
So, that's something you really ought to
think about.

00:43:37.290 --> 00:43:42.670
The second important point I want to make
is, I want to focus on number four.

00:43:42.670 --> 00:43:44.210
Called cut cognitive load.

00:43:46.170 --> 00:43:48.400
So, one of the most famous articles in
Psychology is by

00:43:48.400 --> 00:43:51.550
George Midler, it's called the Magic
Number 7, Plus or Minus 2.

00:43:51.550 --> 00:43:55.930
And, the idea is that there are seven
things we can actually keep in our head.

00:43:57.010 --> 00:44:00.190
And, that's the, that's what we can keep
in working

00:44:00.190 --> 00:44:04.120
memory, that's what we can actually
process in terms of attention.

00:44:04.120 --> 00:44:07.210
You actually increase the load to be on
seven.

00:44:07.210 --> 00:44:11.280
What happens is, willpower is amazingly
eroded.

00:44:12.330 --> 00:44:16.700
SO here is a lovely experiment done by Roy
Bormeester and his colleagues.

00:44:16.700 --> 00:44:20.780
They took a large group like you and
randomly put you into two conditions.

00:44:20.780 --> 00:44:26.759
So both groups, of course, they don't know
that the other group is there, They're

00:44:26.759 --> 00:44:31.470
given ten math problems, and you have to
answer them, in exactly the same sequence.

00:44:31.470 --> 00:44:34.040
Unbeknownst to the participants in the
experiment

00:44:34.040 --> 00:44:35.840
you can only answer four of the ten.

00:44:35.840 --> 00:44:38.610
In the ten minutes given to you.

00:44:38.610 --> 00:44:40.615
So what they experimenters do, is they

00:44:40.615 --> 00:44:42.984
actually tell people in this one group,
hey,

00:44:42.984 --> 00:44:45.050
before you solve the ten math problems,

00:44:45.050 --> 00:44:47.508
just write a two paragraph essay, some
topic.

00:44:47.508 --> 00:44:52.129
Hypothetically, let's assume it's the City
of New York, write an essay without,

00:44:52.129 --> 00:44:56.319
you know, but make sure you don't use
words that actually have the letters.

00:44:56.319 --> 00:44:58.971
Z, or X, which is easy, cuz a lot of

00:44:58.971 --> 00:45:04.630
English words don't have this particular
conjoining of the letters.

00:45:04.630 --> 00:45:09.550
This group, the [UNKNOWN] cognitive load,
they say, write a story about

00:45:09.550 --> 00:45:13.300
New York, but don't use the letter N and
the letter A.

00:45:14.640 --> 00:45:18.270
That's tough, and then please solve the
math problems.

00:45:18.270 --> 00:45:22.017
So everybody does that and the
experimenters go back to these people and

00:45:22.017 --> 00:45:25.890
say, you know what, instead of like
getting each of your answer sheets,

00:45:25.890 --> 00:45:29.949
physically verifying it and giving you a
dollar for each correct answer, why

00:45:29.949 --> 00:45:33.720
don't we scream the answers, and you tell
us how many you got right.

00:45:33.720 --> 00:45:34.350
We'll give you a buck.

00:45:35.750 --> 00:45:38.460
But this is where the experimenters become
very clever.

00:45:38.460 --> 00:45:42.920
So, they actually make sure in each room
there is a paper shredder.

00:45:42.920 --> 00:45:46.820
So, they tell the participants take your
physical answer, shred it.

00:45:46.820 --> 00:45:49.380
Nobody will know how many you got right,
and tell us how

00:45:49.380 --> 00:45:52.430
many you think you got right, and we'll
pay you a buck.

00:45:52.430 --> 00:45:54.810
They do this for this group as well.

00:45:54.810 --> 00:45:58.604
Now this group that was asked to write an
essay without using the letters

00:45:58.604 --> 00:46:02.030
N and A, how many answers do you think
they claim they got right?

00:46:02.030 --> 00:46:04.490
And remember, you can only get four right.

00:46:06.520 --> 00:46:09.650
The average number they claim they got
right was eight.

00:46:09.650 --> 00:46:12.127
[LAUGH] And what about that group there?

00:46:12.127 --> 00:46:16.213
4.75, now, because you randomly assigned
people, it's not

00:46:16.213 --> 00:46:20.130
the dishonest people over here and honest
people over there.

00:46:20.130 --> 00:46:24.760
You know, you've randomized with respect
to inclination towards honesty.

00:46:24.760 --> 00:46:29.110
What happened in this room, what happened
was

00:46:29.110 --> 00:46:33.120
they increased load, they put temptation
in their way.

00:46:34.300 --> 00:46:37.020
What funded the temptation [INAUDIBLE] the
paper shredder.

00:46:38.130 --> 00:46:40.260
And once you use the shredder you can tell

00:46:40.260 --> 00:46:42.550
me how many answers you think you got
right.

00:46:42.550 --> 00:46:45.460
I'm gonna give you a buck for every
correct answer.

00:46:45.460 --> 00:46:49.720
For four extra bucks, basically they
cheated and they lied.

00:46:49.720 --> 00:46:50.640
And, why is that?

00:46:52.200 --> 00:46:55.417
It's not that people are saying, oh my God
this, this was a hard

00:46:55.417 --> 00:46:59.189
task that's why I'm trying to get more
money out of you or something like.

00:47:00.220 --> 00:47:03.690
When you, what is it you need to actually
resist temptation?

00:47:05.960 --> 00:47:08.100
Will power, that's exactly right.

00:47:08.100 --> 00:47:11.640
Most of us think will power is here, brain
power is

00:47:11.640 --> 00:47:17.970
here, but they're actually much more
interdependent than we ever have realized.

00:47:17.970 --> 00:47:20.430
And, what do we do in companies?

00:47:20.430 --> 00:47:21.410
To our employees.

00:47:21.410 --> 00:47:25.480
What do we do in terms of their brain, the
demands on their brain power?

00:47:25.480 --> 00:47:26.760
Is that going up or going down?

00:47:28.100 --> 00:47:28.630
Going up!

00:47:29.640 --> 00:47:30.450
And then what do we do?

00:47:30.450 --> 00:47:33.680
We sit and stand in front of them and say
use your will power.

00:47:33.680 --> 00:47:34.960
You think they have any to use?

00:47:36.510 --> 00:47:37.010
Obviously not.

00:47:38.140 --> 00:47:40.710
And what a lot of executives therefore.

00:47:40.710 --> 00:47:43.184
The smart ones do is they're constantly

00:47:43.184 --> 00:47:46.880
thinking about, how do you actually lower
load?

00:47:46.880 --> 00:47:50.030
How do you actually make sure that the
willpower kicks in?

00:47:50.030 --> 00:47:52.370
Because, remember, excellent
organizations, you don't

00:47:52.370 --> 00:47:53.390
need to tell people what to do.

00:47:53.390 --> 00:47:54.719
They know what the right thing is, they'll
do it.

00:47:56.130 --> 00:47:58.564
So, and for you as you think about your
orga, I

00:47:58.564 --> 00:48:02.300
was just actually at a company who's name
I can't mention.

00:48:02.300 --> 00:48:04.476
And I asked one of their executives, hey
when you had to

00:48:04.476 --> 00:48:07.140
order laptops in your company, how many
people had to approve it?

00:48:07.140 --> 00:48:08.650
He looked at me and said 18 approvals.

00:48:10.290 --> 00:48:11.570
I'm thinking to myself 18 approvals.

00:48:11.570 --> 00:48:14.740
That's a lot of approvals for a laptop
purchase.

00:48:14.740 --> 00:48:15.720
What are most people gonna do?

00:48:15.720 --> 00:48:18.423
They'll give up [UNKNOWN] screw the
laptop, I'm not gonna

00:48:18.423 --> 00:48:20.739
go and get like 18 approvals to do this,
and that

00:48:20.739 --> 00:48:23.053
giving up is the worst kind of problem
you, you can

00:48:23.053 --> 00:48:26.770
have, so you really want to think
carefully about cognitive load.

00:48:26.770 --> 00:48:31.140
The last thing I wanna touch on is, most
people think that going from

00:48:33.240 --> 00:48:36.840
good to, I mean, going towards excellence
is a journey

00:48:36.840 --> 00:48:40.690
where, you know, you just pour in good
into a system.

00:48:40.690 --> 00:48:42.450
And that'll get you to great.

00:48:42.450 --> 00:48:47.843
It turns out that the real thing is bad is
way more powerful than good.

00:48:47.843 --> 00:48:57.900
In fact, a basic finding, in, psychology
is bad dominates good all the time.

00:48:57.900 --> 00:49:00.710
See if I ask you, what are your most
powerful memories.

00:49:00.710 --> 00:49:03.060
Bad memories rush to the forefront.

00:49:03.060 --> 00:49:06.170
What is the most formative relationships,
bad memories

00:49:06.170 --> 00:49:09.420
kind of quickly bad relationships surge to
the forefront.

00:49:09.420 --> 00:49:13.077
In one study of marital couples married
couples they found

00:49:13.077 --> 00:49:15.975
that for every nasty thing you said to
your partner

00:49:15.975 --> 00:49:18.183
you had to at least say a minimum of five

00:49:18.183 --> 00:49:21.640
good things to offset it you know, so you
can imagine.

00:49:22.788 --> 00:49:25.923
Now the point is, if you don't focus on
the

00:49:25.923 --> 00:49:30.420
bad and only focus on the good, you're in
trouble.

00:49:30.420 --> 00:49:34.140
So, the best experiment on bad versus good
was actually

00:49:34.140 --> 00:49:38.670
done by Philip Zimbardo here, that's the
broken windows experiment.

00:49:38.670 --> 00:49:42.336
It wasn't some cop in New York who figured
this out.

00:49:42.336 --> 00:49:45.770
Philip Zimbardo did a brilliant experiment
in 1969.

00:49:45.770 --> 00:49:47.524
He took a VW, put it in Brooklyn,

00:49:47.524 --> 00:49:51.070
broke the windows of the car, and started
filming.

00:49:51.070 --> 00:49:51.900
And guess what?

00:49:51.900 --> 00:49:54.750
If you see the film, you actually have a
father with

00:49:54.750 --> 00:49:57.840
two kids, he sees the broken windows,
quickly steals the radio.

00:49:58.890 --> 00:50:02.360
And within an hour the car's like wiped
clean, ransacked.

00:50:02.360 --> 00:50:04.010
It's become a playground.

00:50:04.010 --> 00:50:06.186
So, Zimbardo says, you know, maybe we

00:50:06.186 --> 00:50:08.618
outta try the experiment in Palo Alto,
after

00:50:08.618 --> 00:50:11.562
all Stanford University, you know, maybe
people are

00:50:11.562 --> 00:50:15.200
better, maybe people are civic minded, et
cetera.

00:50:15.200 --> 00:50:20.080
So, he keeps the card, people are kind of
puzzled they say hey, who's card is it.

00:50:20.080 --> 00:50:22.390
But Zimbardo says, let me break the
windows here too.

00:50:22.390 --> 00:50:25.550
And, you know, he breaks the windows here
too.

00:50:25.550 --> 00:50:27.150
What do you think happens in Palo Alto?

00:50:27.150 --> 00:50:29.830
Exactly the same thing that happened in
Brooklyn.

00:50:29.830 --> 00:50:31.449
What do broken windows tell people?

00:50:33.510 --> 00:50:34.300
That's exactly it.

00:50:34.300 --> 00:50:39.110
Nobody cares, and that's what you've got
to really focus on first.

00:50:39.110 --> 00:50:45.640
So, there's a lovely case of this Chinese
refrigerator company called Haier.

00:50:45.640 --> 00:50:49.230
Lousy refrigerators, they were making bad,
lousy refrigerators.

00:50:49.230 --> 00:50:50.621
what would most companies do?

00:50:50.621 --> 00:50:53.173
Let's get black belt, blue belt, green
belt, whatever

00:50:53.173 --> 00:50:55.320
it is, and beat you them, you know, et
cetera.

00:50:55.320 --> 00:50:56.880
You over invest in them.

00:50:56.880 --> 00:50:58.120
But this guy said wait a minute.

00:50:58.120 --> 00:51:01.182
Max doing all of this statistical quality
control in

00:51:01.182 --> 00:51:05.560
TQM, when the bathrooms the workers are
using are terrible.

00:51:05.560 --> 00:51:07.980
That's the broken window I need to fix.

00:51:07.980 --> 00:51:11.480
Cuz what do the lousy bathrooms tell the
workers?

00:51:11.480 --> 00:51:12.710
Nobody cares.

00:51:12.710 --> 00:51:15.340
And how can you ask them to care for the
customer?

00:51:15.340 --> 00:51:17.180
So, that's where he started.

00:51:17.180 --> 00:51:19.733
So, the real question for you is, you've
got

00:51:19.733 --> 00:51:22.690
to always ask yourself, what is your
broken window?

00:51:22.690 --> 00:51:25.326
And one other piece of interesting
research.

00:51:25.326 --> 00:51:30.309
There are a number of you know, women in
the group here,

00:51:30.309 --> 00:51:34.540
and I know the WIN folks just came in late
as well.

00:51:34.540 --> 00:51:37.620
Particularly for women executives, the
scaling challenge is

00:51:37.620 --> 00:51:40.710
always more likely to be from bad to
great.

00:51:40.710 --> 00:51:44.420
And the reason is, because of something
that is called a glass cliff.

00:51:45.626 --> 00:51:48.694
It turns out that a glass cliff is
obviously

00:51:48.694 --> 00:51:52.140
very different than a glass ceiling and so
on.

00:51:52.140 --> 00:51:54.200
Terms, I'm sure all of you are familiar
with.

00:51:54.200 --> 00:51:57.210
The problem with the glass cliff is, women
are actually

00:51:57.210 --> 00:52:01.260
given leadership opportunities where the
chance of failure is very high.

00:52:03.080 --> 00:52:07.240
So, you know, their given actually,
precarious jobs.

00:52:07.240 --> 00:52:12.310
Stuff to sort of succeed in these jobs,
one reason is, the men have declined them.

00:52:12.310 --> 00:52:14.014
You know, which is one reason.

00:52:14.014 --> 00:52:16.879
You know, they, they, the get, these jobs,
and the woman

00:52:16.879 --> 00:52:19.840
accept them, because their not sure they
might get a second chance.

00:52:19.840 --> 00:52:24.392
The results are experimental evidence that
suggests that people, when

00:52:24.392 --> 00:52:27.601
they ask women to be leaders in situations
where the

00:52:27.601 --> 00:52:31.332
performance is poor, usually when you
think of crises they

00:52:31.332 --> 00:52:35.690
actually gravitate towards a more female
stereotype of a manager.

00:52:35.690 --> 00:52:40.127
That's the experimental kind of evidence,
the long and short

00:52:40.127 --> 00:52:43.052
of it is, I was, of course, giving a talk

00:52:45.250 --> 00:52:47.983
To a group of three hundred women
executives

00:52:47.983 --> 00:52:50.078
of course I was telling them, them this

00:52:50.078 --> 00:52:53.696
and they said of course we know this you
know, we, we they call it the

00:52:53.696 --> 00:52:55.856
glass cliff we've known this for a while

00:52:55.856 --> 00:52:58.776
you know, but the challenges you always
have

00:52:58.776 --> 00:53:03.130
to figure out how is it you're going to
start from the bad to the great.

00:53:03.130 --> 00:53:07.520
We have I thought I'd give a little more
time for questions, but I'm a professor.

00:53:07.520 --> 00:53:10.542
I got caught up in my own, the little
sharing of the book

00:53:10.542 --> 00:53:14.023
idea, so pardon me for that, but if we
have time, let's take a

00:53:14.023 --> 00:53:18.429
couple of questions, and then I'll make a
quick closing remark, and then will

00:53:18.429 --> 00:53:23.120
you know, you can head on to wine and food
and merriment and enjoyment.

00:53:23.120 --> 00:53:26.539
So, any questions that you might like to
ask?

00:53:26.539 --> 00:53:32.792
&gt;&gt; What was about the implications from
feature expert obviously,

00:53:32.792 --> 00:53:38.485
that might be viewed as a model, and about
scaling assets?

00:53:38.485 --> 00:53:40.055
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:53:40.055 --> 00:53:42.324
The fact that she was actually saying I am

00:53:42.324 --> 00:53:46.140
not going to wait for anybody, I'll do it
myself.

00:53:46.140 --> 00:53:47.500
That's what real excellence is.

00:53:49.120 --> 00:53:52.090
So, you, the people who are excellent,
that's kind of what they do.

00:53:52.090 --> 00:53:55.700
Let me, one of the heroes in our book, and
thank you for asking that question.

00:53:55.700 --> 00:53:58.730
One of the heroes in our book is a guy
called Doug Deets.

00:53:58.730 --> 00:54:02.020
Doug is a senior executive in GE health
care.

00:54:02.020 --> 00:54:04.826
You go to his office he's got plaques,

00:54:04.826 --> 00:54:08.482
awards, but that one day went to a
pediatric,

00:54:08.482 --> 00:54:12.480
radiology section and you had young kids
afflicted

00:54:12.480 --> 00:54:16.070
with cancer who actually were, had to have
MRIs.

00:54:17.080 --> 00:54:20.790
And Doug sees these young children
screaming with fear.

00:54:20.790 --> 00:54:23.310
Nearly all of them had to be sedated.

00:54:23.310 --> 00:54:25.360
And he says, is this the kind of product
I'm making?

00:54:26.540 --> 00:54:29.610
That's scaring young kids, and they have
to be sedated.

00:54:29.610 --> 00:54:31.270
Parents have to entrust their seven year

00:54:31.270 --> 00:54:33.810
old to somebody, who's going to sedate
them.

00:54:33.810 --> 00:54:35.220
So what did Dough do?

00:54:35.220 --> 00:54:40.650
Doug was one of the first, edition of our
customer focused innovation program.

00:54:40.650 --> 00:54:43.050
The guy goes back spends time in,

00:54:43.050 --> 00:54:47.750
in daycare and kindergarten, observing
young children.

00:54:47.750 --> 00:54:51.120
And then it dawns on him and he says, oh
my God I've been stupid.

00:54:51.120 --> 00:54:56.110
I've been developing an engineers
solution, to a childs problem of anxiety.

00:54:56.110 --> 00:55:00.160
I need to develop a childs solution, to
the childs problem of anxiety.

00:55:00.160 --> 00:55:01.360
And what does he do?

00:55:01.360 --> 00:55:03.880
In the new line of, things that he's come
up with,

00:55:03.880 --> 00:55:06.470
and he had to fight against a lot of
people in GE.

00:55:06.470 --> 00:55:07.940
Who were saying, do we need to do this?

00:55:07.940 --> 00:55:08.760
Do we not?

00:55:08.760 --> 00:55:10.270
Why and where for?

00:55:10.270 --> 00:55:14.803
So, in the new line of products, what's
amazing is, if you go to an MRI,

00:55:14.803 --> 00:55:20.045
one of the MRI, the, the front end of the
MRI, actually looks like a space capsule.

00:55:20.045 --> 00:55:22.030
[SOUND] So, you tell a young kid, hey.

00:55:22.030 --> 00:55:24.740
You're going inside a space capsule.

00:55:24.740 --> 00:55:26.640
They've forgotten it's narrow.

00:55:26.640 --> 00:55:28.660
That it's actually claustrophobic.

00:55:28.660 --> 00:55:29.810
You're kind of saying, you know?

00:55:29.810 --> 00:55:31.450
Okay, that sounds cool.

00:55:31.450 --> 00:55:32.290
So what, you know?

00:55:32.290 --> 00:55:32.720
Another one.

00:55:32.720 --> 00:55:35.180
There's an x-ray machine that shakes a
lot.

00:55:35.180 --> 00:55:39.190
And, so what they've done is they've
redesigned it, as, like, the rapids.

00:55:39.190 --> 00:55:41.080
So, they tell people, hey, you're in a
raft.

00:55:41.080 --> 00:55:42.580
It's going to go over the rapids.

00:55:42.580 --> 00:55:44.100
It's gonna shake like hell.

00:55:44.100 --> 00:55:48.069
But, it's gonna be fun and, you know, they
do all these little things and

00:55:48.069 --> 00:55:51.033
it's extraordinary what happened to
sedation rates,

00:55:51.033 --> 00:55:53.320
they actually fell down by a large margin.

00:55:55.060 --> 00:55:57.273
Customer satisfaction went up and, of

00:55:57.273 --> 00:56:00.357
course, you know, processing times
improved,

00:56:00.357 --> 00:56:04.660
then got promotions, and as we were
writing this book Bob and I.

00:56:04.660 --> 00:56:05.760
Were on the phone with Doug.

00:56:05.760 --> 00:56:09.980
And, you know, Doug, what's gonna happen
to you, now that you've become a VP?

00:56:09.980 --> 00:56:11.980
And he says, no I just declined the
promotion.

00:56:11.980 --> 00:56:14.680
I said what?

00:56:14.680 --> 00:56:16.650
Why did you decline the promotion?

00:56:16.650 --> 00:56:20.450
And Doug said well, I came home one day.

00:56:23.030 --> 00:56:24.800
And my wife had just come back from a
mammogram.

00:56:26.190 --> 00:56:32.040
And like most males, I sorta asked her,
honey, is everything okay?

00:56:32.040 --> 00:56:33.070
Was it okay?

00:56:33.070 --> 00:56:36.790
And Doug's wife is definitely a pistol.

00:56:36.790 --> 00:56:40.255
So, she kinda quickly realized that Doug
needed

00:56:40.255 --> 00:56:43.183
some serious user feedback to put it
mildly.

00:56:43.183 --> 00:56:46.490
[LAUGH] So she looked at Doug and said.

00:56:46.490 --> 00:56:50.890
Come, lemmi show you what it feels like.

00:56:50.890 --> 00:56:53.230
[LAUGH] So, yeah, I know, I know.

00:56:53.230 --> 00:56:54.710
Poor Doug, you know?

00:56:54.710 --> 00:56:56.480
Unsuspecting Doug.

00:56:56.480 --> 00:56:58.940
He's actually led into the garage.

00:56:58.940 --> 00:57:03.880
She opens the garage door, and says, Doug,
put your thing out.

00:57:03.880 --> 00:57:05.950
I'll show you what it feels like.

00:57:05.950 --> 00:57:08.020
[LAUGH] And Doug says, oh my God.

00:57:09.510 --> 00:57:11.770
And he, he looks at her, and says, message
received.

00:57:13.900 --> 00:57:15.590
I actually, I'm gonna focus on this.

00:57:15.590 --> 00:57:18.390
And that's what he's been doing for the
last year and a half.

00:57:18.390 --> 00:57:19.470
What's he been doing?

00:57:19.470 --> 00:57:21.530
Coming out with a new range of
mammography,

00:57:23.630 --> 00:57:26.980
environments, where you actually can
[UNKNOWN].

00:57:26.980 --> 00:57:28.820
And the images are much better.

00:57:28.820 --> 00:57:32.020
You don't need to face the excruciating
pain, pressure, all of

00:57:32.020 --> 00:57:34.050
these kinds of things that a lot of people
go through.

00:57:34.050 --> 00:57:36.960
So, when I think of that young school
teacher, it's like,

00:57:36.960 --> 00:57:39.360
you don't wait for people to tell you what
to do.

00:57:39.360 --> 00:57:41.880
You do sort of what you need to.

00:57:41.880 --> 00:57:43.550
Yes, please.

00:57:43.550 --> 00:57:44.220
Right there.

00:57:48.110 --> 00:57:51.125
&gt;&gt; You talked a little earlier about the
premortem.

00:57:51.125 --> 00:57:51.439
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:57:51.439 --> 00:57:55.495
&gt;&gt; As an idea, where people kinda write
down how they would fail.

00:57:55.495 --> 00:57:57.860
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm, or how they would succeed, too.

00:57:57.860 --> 00:58:00.047
&gt;&gt; Yes, how do you prevent the people

00:58:00.047 --> 00:58:03.375
who write the failure condition from
freaking out?

00:58:03.375 --> 00:58:07.801
[LAUGH] Or you know, maybe fulfilling the
prophecy themselves?

00:58:07.801 --> 00:58:11.710
&gt;&gt; so, that's a reasonable question.

00:58:11.710 --> 00:58:14.940
So what you want is you want to stimulate
their imagination, but you don't

00:58:14.940 --> 00:58:18.580
want to arouse their anxiety is kind of
the other way you put it.

00:58:18.580 --> 00:58:20.059
So, what you have to do is you

00:58:20.059 --> 00:58:23.274
actually have to give them reasonably
detailed instructions

00:58:23.274 --> 00:58:28.550
that serve as kind of mental scaffolding,
so they're not gonna go off the deep end.

00:58:28.550 --> 00:58:31.180
So, what you want is you actually, so for

00:58:31.180 --> 00:58:34.190
instance, you would say, here's a story
that you, when

00:58:34.190 --> 00:58:35.750
you fail, this is the kind of story that's

00:58:35.750 --> 00:58:40.600
gonna feature in the pages of NYT or
whatever magazine.

00:58:40.600 --> 00:58:42.020
And then you say, write a story.

00:58:43.210 --> 00:58:46.180
And when you write a story, you actually
give them a page limit.

00:58:46.180 --> 00:58:48.830
You know, so you, you don't need to go on
and on.

00:58:48.830 --> 00:58:51.130
And you tell them you can write the story
any way you want.

00:58:51.130 --> 00:58:52.070
You can write it as a memo.

00:58:52.070 --> 00:58:56.120
You can write it as whatever, but it has
to be a story and not a list.

00:58:56.120 --> 00:58:58.260
And usually what people do is they

00:58:58.260 --> 00:59:00.490
actually give pretty granular kinds of
accounts.

00:59:01.500 --> 00:59:04.320
Now, I found that the stories of failure.

00:59:04.320 --> 00:59:06.780
Or actually little more concrete than
stories

00:59:06.780 --> 00:59:08.880
of success, and the reason you need

00:59:08.880 --> 00:59:14.100
to do both is, what causes failure may be
quite different from what causes success.

00:59:14.100 --> 00:59:16.440
It's not like there are five factors, high
on

00:59:16.440 --> 00:59:20.042
everything success, low on everything
failure you know, it doesn't

00:59:20.042 --> 00:59:22.923
work like that, you know life's too
complicated to work

00:59:22.923 --> 00:59:25.990
like that, and, so you have to take those
things.

00:59:25.990 --> 00:59:28.257
You then have to put them together, and
then

00:59:28.257 --> 00:59:31.410
you have to analyze what the common themes
are.

00:59:31.410 --> 00:59:35.133
I usually like to after reading when I
work with companies, I

00:59:35.133 --> 00:59:39.148
usually gather all these premortem, and I
present them not in the

00:59:39.148 --> 00:59:42.798
sense of, like you know, so many people
said this or that,

00:59:42.798 --> 00:59:47.370
but you present them more in the visual of
a downward spiral.

00:59:47.370 --> 00:59:49.376
And then an upward spiral, people say oh

00:59:49.376 --> 00:59:51.800
my God, that's where we need to start
earning.

00:59:51.800 --> 00:59:53.442
&gt;&gt; Do the same people write both stories?

00:59:53.442 --> 00:59:56.974
&gt;&gt; No, you have to actually, cuz once
you've imagined one thing,.

00:59:56.974 --> 00:59:59.563
It's hard to reimagine the other, so you,
you

00:59:59.563 --> 01:00:01.762
got to make it random and not say, well,

01:00:01.762 --> 01:00:05.930
let me go to the optimist and let me go to
the pessimist and that kind of thing.

01:00:05.930 --> 01:00:07.220
You don't want to do all that.

01:00:07.220 --> 01:00:12.121
Just make it random, one page, and
anonymous, independent, they

01:00:12.121 --> 01:00:16.870
actually ask, you can actually get a lot
of useful things.

01:00:16.870 --> 01:00:21.970
By the way, you might wanna do that if
you're thinking of taking new jobs, too.

01:00:21.970 --> 01:00:26.410
So you should really do a pre-mortem of
the job and ask the person hiring you,

01:00:26.410 --> 01:00:29.150
or if you're the person hiring somebody,
you

01:00:29.150 --> 01:00:31.370
know, you can actually do this sort of
interchanging.

01:00:31.370 --> 01:00:33.890
Cuz that's really what you wanna negotiate
about,

01:00:33.890 --> 01:00:35.990
not simply how much you're gonna be paid.

01:00:35.990 --> 01:00:37.990
Cuz everything's about the runway.

01:00:37.990 --> 01:00:42.300
Is it a short runway, is it a long runway,
how to prepare the runway.

01:00:42.300 --> 01:00:44.210
One more question please.

01:00:44.210 --> 01:00:46.110
Then we're gonna be, out of time.

01:00:46.110 --> 01:00:47.590
Yep.

01:00:47.590 --> 01:00:49.976
&gt;&gt; I read Marty Seligman's book which
references

01:00:49.976 --> 01:00:51.820
the five to one ratio for couples, and he

01:00:51.820 --> 01:00:53.772
also talks about how there's a three to
one

01:00:53.772 --> 01:00:57.480
ratio for organizations, of positive
comments to negative comments.

01:00:57.480 --> 01:01:00.289
So I've implemented this in my
organization, but one of

01:01:00.289 --> 01:01:03.331
the questions that I've always had is, if
people in meetings

01:01:03.331 --> 01:01:06.549
sit around telling each other how
fantastic they are, then there

01:01:06.549 --> 01:01:10.460
can be a demotivating effect of basically
being average or okay.

01:01:10.460 --> 01:01:13.930
So is there any experimental evidence of
a, of a top side to that

01:01:13.930 --> 01:01:16.418
ratio of when organizations are too
positive

01:01:16.418 --> 01:01:19.430
and the employees don't feel enough
motivation?

01:01:19.430 --> 01:01:21.180
&gt;&gt; That's an excellent question.

01:01:21.180 --> 01:01:27.147
I think what you don't want to do is, you
know, Seligman's view, of course, is it's

01:01:27.147 --> 01:01:31.425
all about positive thinking and positive
emotion and there's

01:01:31.425 --> 01:01:35.660
certainly quite a bit to kinda recommend
about that.

01:01:35.660 --> 01:01:37.856
But if you only give people information
about what is it

01:01:37.856 --> 01:01:41.210
that they're good at and you don't talk
about their failure.

01:01:41.210 --> 01:01:43.563
Clearly, you're giving them biased

01:01:43.563 --> 01:01:46.726
information, and paradoxically, that very
bias

01:01:46.726 --> 01:01:51.690
in the information, causes people to
discount the usefulness of it.

01:01:51.690 --> 01:01:54.597
So, what you want to give people is, the
way I sort

01:01:54.597 --> 01:01:59.030
of think about it is, it's not feed back
but feed forward.

01:01:59.030 --> 01:02:02.190
So it's not like what did you do well or
what did you do poorly.

01:02:02.190 --> 01:02:04.215
What is it you need to do in the future?

01:02:04.215 --> 01:02:07.875
Cuz, you know, a little bit of the
rearview glass is a useful thing, but

01:02:07.875 --> 01:02:11.490
you gotta get them to think what is it
they're going to do in the future?

01:02:12.600 --> 01:02:20.540
And you know, I think when you think of
the negative things.

01:02:20.540 --> 01:02:22.822
Here the important thing, of course, is
not

01:02:22.822 --> 01:02:25.476
to fall prey to the fundamental
attribution adder.

01:02:25.476 --> 01:02:34.712
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
the number is too high?

01:02:34.712 --> 01:02:34.791
&gt;&gt; Uh-huh.

01:02:34.791 --> 01:02:35.416
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:02:35.416 --> 01:02:37.746
I don't think that there's kind of upper

01:02:37.746 --> 01:02:40.690
bound if that's kind of what you're
referring to.

01:02:41.950 --> 01:02:45.800
Our interest of course, in the bad being
stronger than good

01:02:45.800 --> 01:02:48.220
is I sort of think of that as a nice
study.

01:02:48.220 --> 01:02:53.930
That tells you how potent bad is and why
you need to pay attention to it.

01:02:53.930 --> 01:02:59.890
I think a lot of it is also quite honestly
the kinds of people you hire.

01:03:00.930 --> 01:03:04.630
I was actually with the CEO recently and I
said, what's your secret of hiring?

01:03:04.630 --> 01:03:05.770
How do you scale excellence?

01:03:05.770 --> 01:03:08.390
And he says, well, I try and actually look
for

01:03:08.390 --> 01:03:12.330
people who are tougher on themselves than
I am on them.

01:03:12.330 --> 01:03:13.350
I don't need to micromanage it.

01:03:14.650 --> 01:03:16.920
Then I can put an arm around them, you
know.

01:03:16.920 --> 01:03:19.900
Now the catch is, how do you find those
people?

01:03:19.900 --> 01:03:24.180
And sometimes you can find them in the
oddest of spaces.

01:03:24.180 --> 01:03:27.323
One lovely story I heard from a hedge fund
executive in New York

01:03:27.323 --> 01:03:31.420
was I said, how do you hire people at,
the, the entry level?

01:03:31.420 --> 01:03:34.542
You get to like Math jocks and you know,
so forth, or whizzes

01:03:34.542 --> 01:03:37.920
and so on and so forth, or how do you go
about doing it?

01:03:37.920 --> 01:03:42.360
He says, no, we actually aim to hire
athletes from the best university.

01:03:42.360 --> 01:03:46.540
I said, athletes, like, exactly what does
that tell you?

01:03:47.800 --> 01:03:51.641
He looked at me and he said, the great
thing about athletes from the

01:03:51.641 --> 01:03:53.836
best universities, is they may not be

01:03:53.836 --> 01:03:57.700
eventually succeed in terms of a
professional career.

01:03:57.700 --> 01:04:00.420
But they have two amazing characteristics.

01:04:00.420 --> 01:04:06.520
The first thing is, these athletes have an
incredible capacity to do repetitive work.

01:04:08.950 --> 01:04:10.010
Hundred meter swim.

01:04:11.360 --> 01:04:12.250
Do it again.

01:04:12.250 --> 01:04:12.820
Do it again.

01:04:12.820 --> 01:04:15.110
They can do that for a long period of
time.

01:04:15.110 --> 01:04:18.700
The other, the, the other thing he said,
which was like

01:04:18.700 --> 01:04:23.430
a brilliant line was, he said they're the
most open to feedback.

01:04:23.430 --> 01:04:24.990
They can be coached.

01:04:24.990 --> 01:04:26.160
Cuz what do you do if you're an athlete?

01:04:26.160 --> 01:04:27.010
You get the hundred meters.

01:04:27.010 --> 01:04:29.860
And then you sorta look at the coach and
say, what did I do wrong?

01:04:29.860 --> 01:04:31.400
Where'd I, what do I need to fix?

01:04:31.400 --> 01:04:33.870
So he said, that's why we need to, we hire
people like that.

01:04:33.870 --> 01:04:35.060
Cuz.

01:04:35.060 --> 01:04:36.800
They're actually craving that feedback.

01:04:36.800 --> 01:04:39.730
But they keep, keep on doing the
repetitive work.

01:04:39.730 --> 01:04:43.047
And they're not all thinking they wanna be
you know

01:04:43.047 --> 01:04:47.280
running the company you know, imminently
soon or whatever it is.

01:04:47.280 --> 01:04:50.004
So it's 6:15.

01:04:50.004 --> 01:04:53.570
My hope is this has been useful to you.

01:04:53.570 --> 01:04:55.800
And you wanna actually think of.

01:04:55.800 --> 01:04:59.442
You know, taking some of these ideas about
scaling excellence.

01:04:59.442 --> 01:05:02.992
So as you scale them and if you have a
chance to read the

01:05:02.992 --> 01:05:08.430
book, by all means please write to me with
questions, stories, and the like.

01:05:09.490 --> 01:05:12.040
That's how at least I get educated.

01:05:12.040 --> 01:05:14.040
But I want to close with one little story

01:05:14.040 --> 01:05:16.630
hopefully that kind of inspires you a
little bit.

01:05:16.630 --> 01:05:18.940
And that is some, sometimes when you

01:05:18.940 --> 01:05:22.053
have to scale excellence, you don't have
resources.

01:05:22.053 --> 01:05:24.730
You don't have money, you don't have the
people, you

01:05:24.730 --> 01:05:27.090
don't have all these things, how do you do
that?

01:05:27.090 --> 01:05:30.699
And a great example of that is this chain
here called Cost Plus World

01:05:30.699 --> 01:05:34.823
Market, in California, when the CEO took
over the company, the stock was I

01:05:34.823 --> 01:05:37.529
think at a buck and a half a share, when
he sold it to

01:05:37.529 --> 01:05:42.090
Bed, Bath and Beyond I think he sold it
for twenty two dollars a share.

01:05:42.090 --> 01:05:45.420
So I asked Barry, I said, Barry, how did
you turn this place around?

01:05:45.420 --> 01:05:48.360
I said, did you pay out big bonuses?

01:05:48.360 --> 01:05:50.450
He said, can't do that, no money to do
that.

01:05:50.450 --> 01:05:51.670
Did you hire great people?

01:05:51.670 --> 01:05:52.170
Can't do that.

01:05:53.660 --> 01:05:54.460
Did you train them?

01:05:54.460 --> 01:05:55.820
He said, how can I train people?

01:05:55.820 --> 01:05:59.134
We sell, you know, jam from Sweden to
handy crafts from Kenya

01:05:59.134 --> 01:06:04.090
to whatever, nobody can have all the
product knowledge, it's too complicated.

01:06:04.090 --> 01:06:04.970
So, I said, what did you do?

01:06:06.600 --> 01:06:10.709
And he said like, well, we figured out
that the real task is to

01:06:10.709 --> 01:06:15.810
get people to buy more than one thing when
they walk into a store.

01:06:15.810 --> 01:06:16.510
So I said, what did you do?

01:06:16.510 --> 01:06:17.950
Did you have an incentive program?

01:06:17.950 --> 01:06:19.660
The more people bought, the more people
got paid?

01:06:19.660 --> 01:06:20.820
He said no.

01:06:20.820 --> 01:06:23.290
I said, what if you do, then?

01:06:23.290 --> 01:06:25.550
He said, we told our employees to do two
things.

01:06:26.830 --> 01:06:28.560
When a customer walks in, smile.

01:06:30.090 --> 01:06:34.100
And the second thing is, when a customer
walks in, give him or her a basket.

01:06:37.970 --> 01:06:38.890
That's it.

01:06:38.890 --> 01:06:40.320
That's what you need to do.

01:06:40.320 --> 01:06:43.794
Because the moment you give them a basket,
the probability

01:06:43.794 --> 01:06:47.840
of them purchasing more than one product
has shot up substantially.

01:06:48.890 --> 01:06:50.380
Then I'm gonna just buy one thing.

01:06:50.380 --> 01:06:52.520
They'll probably buy three things.

01:06:52.520 --> 01:06:55.800
It was the most effective thing that we
did, he said.

01:06:55.800 --> 01:07:01.036
And my question to you, as you're thinking
about scaling up excellence in

01:07:01.036 --> 01:07:06.534
your own respective organizations is,
what's the equal end of your basket?

01:07:06.534 --> 01:07:08.672
So think about that.

01:07:08.672 --> 01:07:10.929
It's 6:15, I'm out of time.

01:07:10.929 --> 01:07:12.247
Thank you so very much.

01:07:12.247 --> 01:07:15.044
[SOUND] Thank you.

01:07:15.044 --> 01:07:19.849
[MUSIC]

